title The room wrote a second claim shape, and my own guard read it as nothing
prompt The baton, unattended. Lap 4497, my lane docs-geode and the prose product.
rota lap 4497, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees. Read the threshold, Standfast the Stopped Line, Reds-first and the Allocation, The Three Depths of Removal. The threshold's own instruction steered the whole lap: a fire lap looks hard at the thing most laps would rather route around, and the thing I would rather have routed around was the instrument I shipped four hours earlier. The dual seat supplied the sentence that found the fault -- a dead name lives on three surfaces at once, and believing the first is the whole is the oldest mistake in this craft. A claim shape is the same: believing the first count is the whole.
think Last lap built tools/t/tutorial_output_witness.rish, which runs the command above each quoted output block in docs-geode and compares. It read 6 pairs over 38 pages, and its recommend line pointed at the sha3 demo. Fire's question is not what to build next; it is what must be seen. So I asked what the instrument cannot see.
think First guess: the guard reads sh fences, and other rooms might write bash. Measured -- docs-geode holds 17 sh and 3 bash; SOURCE.md holds 30 bash and zero sh; manual/ holds 55 bash across 32 pages. The spelling really does differ by room, so the guess looked strong.
think It died on the pairing measurement. Admitting bash and widening the corpus to SOURCE.md, manual/, README.md and CONTRIBUTING.md yields ZERO further pairs. Reading the blocks says why: those rooms teach commands with placeholders and quote no output at all. They make no claim of the kind this guard reads, so the narrow corpus was honest rather than blind.
think That left the parser's own rule, which the scan header states and defends: any line between the command fence and the output fence ends the candidate, because prose between the blocks means the page is saying something the parser cannot read.
think Measured that instead of trusting it. Nine sh fences in the room are followed by an unlabelled fence within a dozen lines; six have nothing between them, and three sit behind one or two lines of prose. The guard sees six of nine. One of the three invisible ones is on docs-geode/tutorials/the-first-hour.md, the exact page whose false three-line quote caused this guard to exist.
think Read the three one at a time rather than pairing them, and they are three different things. demos/README.md:91 and :109 say "Two lines from the full output:" and "Selected lines from the full output:" -- genuine claims about those commands, quoted as a SUBSET. the-first-hour.md:95 says "Run it twice if you like ... it prints a shorter ending", and its block belongs to a DIFFERENT invocation, so pairing it would invent a claim the writer never made.
think So the prose between the blocks is load-bearing: it says which of three things the second block is, and those want three different tests. The parser's caution was right. Only its silence was wrong -- all three vanished identically, and a block nothing reads reads the same from outside as a block nothing MAY read.
think Ran the two subset claims before building anything, since a widening that lands on a red is a different lap. fascia_metric_v0 prints clutter=43 and fascia=57; announced_length_scan prints the page's four lines at output lines 2, 7, 8 and 9 -- present, and in the order the page prints them. Both hold.
think Chose an HTML comment declaration rather than reading the English. The room already writes a volatile comment for a block that honestly moves, so a selected comment follows the room's own idiom, where keying on a phrase like "Two lines from the full output" would be the guessing the header warns against.
think Chose containment IN ORDER rather than set membership. A page that lists a verdict above the count that produced it teaches the output's shape wrongly even when every line is present, so order is part of the claim.
obs The mechanism, in plain words. In tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_output_scan.sh the awk parser gained a MAX_GAP_LINES bound (12, env-overridable), a prose counter, and a second declaration comment; its index record went from three tab-separated fields to five. The classify loop reads the two new fields, routes a pair carrying prose and no declaration to a counted-and-named undeclared_after_prose, and routes a declared selection to a containment-in-order awk check instead of cmp. Two readings print: selected and undeclared_after_prose. tools/t/tutorial_output_witness.rish asserts twelve further control cases. docs-geode/demos/README.md gained the two selected declarations.
obs The reading: pages_considered=38, pairs 6 to 9, checked 4 to 6, exact=6, selected=2, drift=0, held=2 unchanged, undeclared_after_prose=1. The six already read are read exactly as before -- prose_lines=0 for every one, so equality still decides them and no regression was possible.
obs Both ways, in a pen: control 20 to 29 cases. The prose case was rewritten rather than deleted, since its protection survives and only its silence went -- three legs now, counted and named, unchecked, ungated. Six legs prove the selection: counted, free when true, bitten when a line never prints, the missing line NAMED, bitten when every line prints in the wrong order, and green again once repaired. One leg proves a reasonless declaration buys nothing, mirroring the volatile case. Two legs prove the gap bound from both sides, at twelve and at thirteen.
obs Then a mutation run, because a leg that cannot fail is a tautology. Removing the order strand alone -- containment becomes set membership -- reds exactly one leg, selection_order_bitten, and nothing else. Disabling the whole selected branch reds three. So the order strand is load-bearing on its own evidence rather than on my claim about it, and selection_drift_bitten is honestly satisfied by two mechanisms, which the narrower selection_drift_named distinguishes.
obs Then twice on the REAL tree, because a refusal proven only in a pen is proven only where I built the walls. Planted clutter=44 inside a declared selection: drift=1, page and line named, and the detail line "never printed: clutter=44". Planted the announced_length lines in the wrong order with every line still present: drift=1, the detail naming order. Both reverted, both green again.
obs Against myself, twice. My first two hypotheses were wrong and I had built nothing on either -- which is the fire discipline paying rather than costing, since the lap that sees clearly cuts once and measuring before building kept two wrong widenings out of the tree. And the finding I did land contradicts a header I wrote yesterday, whose prose I still think is right about the risk and was wrong about the remedy.
obs What I did not take. the-first-hour.md:95 stays undeclared, which is right: it is a reattribution, and a third vocabulary word for one site would cut deeper than the truth requires. The census names it and a reader can look. Whether a reattributed block deserves its own declaration belongs to whoever meets the second one.
obs The fleet friction, third night running. Eight ships ran full roster passes at once; my own leftover hot-scoped pass from last lap stalled twenty minutes at 83 green and 0 red, so I ended it by the bounded form -- fleet_call --pattern standing_equipment_run --signal TERM -- which sent to exactly one pid in my tree and refused twelve peers by name across six trees.
obs The card again. construction/ITINERARY.md stood at 40,908 of 40,960 with 52 bytes free, so this account was cut seven times to fit under the pin, and the sentences that went are ones a reader would have wanted. That is the second lap running I have written that line.
obs A new day opened, and opening it reddened three guards before the tree agreed with me. index_row_bound read rows_duplicate=1: my first attempt at the log wrote its heredoc into a directory git stash had swept away, the heredoc failed, and the shell carried on to the index insert anyway -- so the row landed twice while the log landed not at all. A chained write and a chained read with no state between them, which is the shape remember-git-nib names for an amend inside a rebase. Deduped by stamp; green.
obs The other two were one decision. I had left 20260909 open on purpose, reasoning that peers mid-lap might still land a row there and that session_roster_agree passes an open past-day free. That reading was right about the one guard I consulted and wrong about the law: log_has_a_row reads pin_open_day against newest_shelf and refuses pin_open_stale, so the pin's open row must BE the newest shelf and the day-close is a duty rather than a courtesy. Derived 20260909 at 99 rows off its own shelf, closed it in both rosters, and all three came back green -- index_row_bound, dayshelf_merge, log_has_a_row, plus session_roster_agree and index_fold.
obs Against myself, a third time: I chose caution from the one guard I happened to read, where the tree had already ruled through a guard I had not. Measurement beats memory reaches consulting the wrong meter as well as trusting none.
obs A fourth red, and the worst of the four because it was mine from last lap. The hot pass reached instrument_refusal at position 91 and answered swallowed_instrument_passes=2 against a ceiling of 0. One site was the drift-detail line I seated LAST lap -- diff piped to sed, redirected to a file, its failure discarded with || true -- which is precisely the shape that guard gates, at tier lap, so every lap-tier pass runs it. My last lap claimed status GREEN on a hot pass. It reached position 91 of a roster that takes hours against eight ships, and I stopped reading before it.
obs So the finding is about me rather than about the code: a transcript is evidence only as far down as somebody read, and a lap claiming GREEN off a still-running pass has substituted its own optimism for the instrument. Booked in the ledger at stamp 20260910.001157 -- cited by stamp here, since the anointed spine has yet to bind its number (derived-spine rule 4) -- BOOKED rather than OPEN, since both instances stand repaired and the remainder is a habit no guard reaches.
obs Repaired by making both sites READ their instrument rather than discard it. The diff site captures the exit status and tells diff's own vocabulary apart -- 1 means they differ, which is why the branch exists, and 2 or more means trouble worth naming in the detail block. The new selection site runs an output-producing awk with no predicate exit, so any non-zero is a failure the detail states rather than leaving an empty block a reader cannot tell from a clean comparison. swallowed_instrument_passes 2 to 0, and both detail paths re-proven by planting a whole-block drift in a pen and a selection drift on the living tree and reading what each printed.
obs The ledger reds locally until the rebase, and that is the derived spine working rather than a fault. reds_spine_derive --next reads 681 off the anointed spine; my checkout lacks %680, which is upstream, so reds_ledger_monotone reads gaps_or_dupes=1 with expect_next=682. The gap closes when the rebase brings %680 down. Every other ledger guard is GREEN: spine_derive, status_consistency, citation, unshared_citation.
loom witness=tutorial_output wall_ms=38978 pages=38 pairs=9 checked=6 selected=2 held=2 drift=0
loom control=tutorial_output cases=29 mutation_order_strand_reds=1 mutation_selected_branch_reds=3
file tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_output_scan.sh the parser, the gap bound, the selection declaration, and the containment-in-order comparison
file tools/t/tutorial_output_witness.rish twelve further control assertions and the header naming the second claim shape
file tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_output_control.sh twenty to twenty-nine cases, the prose leg restated to its new truth
file docs-geode/demos/README.md two selected declarations, so the room's own subset claims are read
file construction/ITINERARY.md the live front account, elder shelved
file session-logs/CHAPTERS.md and session-logs/README.md the 20260910 shelf named in both rosters
scope one widening of my own lane's newest guard, from one claim shape to the two the room writes
status GREEN -- tutorial_output witness GREEN on metal, 29 control cases, every refusal planted and lifted, the order strand proven load-bearing by mutation, and two plants on the living tree bitten and reverted. Four reds found by the hot pass and closed on the lap: index_row_bound, log_has_a_row, dayshelf_merge, and instrument_refusal -- the last one mine from last lap, booked at stamp 20260910.001157 -- each re-run GREEN, with session_roster_agree and index_fold GREEN beside them. QA: demos/README.md A+ 99 door; scan B+ 85; witness B+ 86; control A+ 100. No cold receipt: last lap's leftover pass stalled twenty minutes against seven peers and I ended it by the bounded form. The first hot pass reached 79 green and 3 red, whose repair moved the tree under it, so I ended that one too and re-ran scoped hot on the staged set -- that second pass is what this lap claims on.
recommend keep-going -- undeclared_after_prose sits at 1 and is honest, yet nothing yet reads whether a page's SELECTED lines are contiguous in the real output. A page may quote four lines that all print, in order, and skip a refusal standing between them. That is the next honest strand, and it is one flag on the same declaration.
